      {
        "review_id": 681564323,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r681564323-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Very Interesting and a must do",
        "text": "We did enjoy this part of our tour however as foreign tourist and not a Hindu, you cannot enter the main temple complex. We did feel a little frustrated however the experience of seeing the cremation was a very interesting experience.\n\nBe aware that after you past the temple entrance where remember you cannot enter the place is also filthy and there are dog feaces near and around the entrance as any many homeless children just playing on the steps in the dirty water. A UNESCO site and places of worship should be treated with the respect and dignity and like many places were visited during our month long trip this is not the case.\n\nThe pagoda-style Pashupatinath Temple was constructed in 1696, but has been a site of Hindu and Buddhist worship for far longer. The nearby riverside steps are Nepal's most holy cremation site.\n\nDespite being clogged with garbage and black with pollution, the Bagmati River is actually an extremely sacred river. Pashupatinath is the Nepalese equivalent of Varanasi on the sacred River Ganges. The cremation ghats along the Bagmati are the city's most important location for open-air cremations and we were there to see a cremation which was very interesting and weird at the same time. We then walked along the river behind the temple to view the preparation ceremony this was even more strange. But still very intertesting",
        "review_tip": null,
